'''Studley Royal Park''' is an estate in [[North Yorkshire]], England. The site has an area of {{convert|323|ha|acre|order=flip}}<ref>{{cite web |title=National Trust Fountains Abbey and Studley Royal Water Garden |url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/fountains-abbey/ |access-date=20 April 2012 |publisher=National Trust}}</ref> and includes an 18th-century landscaped garden; the ruins of [[Fountains Abbey]]; [[Fountains Hall]], a [[Jacobean architecture|Jacobean]] mansion; and the Victorian [[St Mary's, Studley Royal|St Mary's church]], designed by [[William Burges]]. Studley Royal House, around which the park and gardens were designed, burned down in 1946. The park, as '''Studley Royal Park including the ruins of Fountains Abbey''', has been designated a [[World Heritage Site]]. It has also been designated a [[grade I listed]] park and garden by [[Historic England]], and various structures within it are individually listed. 

After the [[Dissolution of the Monasteries]], the Fountains estate was owned by the Gresham, Proctor, and Messenger families. At the same time, the adjacent Studley estate was separately held by the Mallorie (or Mallory) and then Aislabie families, after the marriage of Mary Mallory and George Aislabie. The estates were combined on 22 December 1767, when William Aislabie purchased the Fountains estate from John Messenger. In 1966, the property came into public ownership after its purchase by [[West Riding County Council]]. In 1983, it was acquired by the [[National Trust]]. 

The gardens and park reflect every stage in the evolution of English garden fashion, from the late 17th century to the 1780s and beyond. Most unusually, both John and William embraced new garden fashions by extending their [[designed landscape]] rather than replacing and remaking outmoded parts. As a result, the cumulative whole is a catalogue of significant landscaping styles.

== Background ==
[[File:Half-moon Pond and River Skell toward Fountains Abbey - geograph.org.uk - 3224224.jpg|thumb|The River Skell runs through the estate.]]
Studley Royal Park is an estate in North Yorkshire, England. The land broadly slopes and east-facing views are a feature of its landscape.<ref name=":0">{{cite book |last=Newman |first=Mark |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8erTrQEACAAJ |title=The Wonder of the North: Fountains Abbey and Studley Royal |date=2015 |publisher=Boydell Press |isbn=978-1-84383-883-8 }}</ref>{{Rp|page=2}} The [[River Skell]] runs through the site, cutting through layers of [[Pennsylvanian (geology)|Upper Carboniferous]] sandstone and Permian Magnesian limestone.<ref>Larwood, Jonathan, and Natural England Palaeontology. [https://www.academia.edu/download/65416437/Culturally_natural_or_naturally_cultural_Final.pdf#page=26 "Geodiversity–a cultural template."] ''Culturally Natural or Naturally Cultural'' (2017): 16–19.</ref> The park was formed through the aggregation of the former land-holdings of [[Fountains Abbey]], which were purchased by the Gresham family after the Dissolution, and the estate of Studley Royal.<ref>{{cite web |title=History of Fountains Abbey │ Yorkshire |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/yorkshire/fountains-abbey-and-studley-royal-water-garden/www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/yorkshire/fountains-abbey-and-studley-royal-water-garden/history-of-fountains-abbey-and-studley-royal |access-date=28 November 2022 |website=National Trust }}</ref>

Whilst the prehistoric origins of the land upon which Studley Royal Park now stands are under-researched, there is evidence for settlement in the area. An excavated flint assemblage from the park demonstrates the presence of people working flint on the site. There is evidence of farming activity dated to 4,500 years ago.<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=4-5}}

Material from the [[British Iron Age|Iron Age]] is also associated with the site, including a lost gold [[torc]]. Iron Age enclosures at Mackershaw date from the sixth to fifth centuries BC. In the later phases of that enclosure, [[Romano-British]] material, including an Egyptian glass bangle, has also been discovered. The presence of Romano-British communities is also attested by the excavation of four skeletons by the vicar of [[Wath (near Ripon)|Wath]] in 1881.<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=6-7}}

Documentary sources and place-name evidence, rather than archaeological excavation, provide insight into the early medieval period in the area. The Venerable [[Bede]] recorded that King [[Alhfrith]] of [[Deira]] granted land for a monastery near [[Ripon]] to Eata.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|8–9}}

== History ==

=== Fountains Abbey estate ===
[[Fountains Abbey]] was founded in 1132 by [[Benedictine]] monks who left [[St Mary's Abbey, York]] to follow the [[Cistercian]] order.<ref name=":1">{{cite web |title=Studley Royal Park including the Ruins of Fountains Abbey – UNESCO World Heritage Centre |url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/372/ |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref> During the medieval period, monastic landholding steadily increased. For example, in the 1220s, [[Cassandra de Aleman]] donated land at Swanley to become part of the monastic grange.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|24}}

After the [[dissolution of the monasteries]] in 1539 by [[Henry VIII]], the Abbey buildings and over 40% of the former monastic estate was sold by the Crown to Sir [[Richard Gresham]], a merchant.<ref name=":1" /> The Greshams, as new owners of a formerly monastic site had a responsibility to render it incapable of future religious use. This was done through a programme of demolition and sale of goods, which included the stripping of lead from the buildings, the removal of glass and [[Crinoidal limestone|Nidderdale 'marble']] from the church.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|37–39}}

The property was passed down through several generations of Sir Richard's family, then sold to [[Stephen Proctor]] in 1596. This included the precinct, Fountains Park and Swanley Grange.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|44–45}} It was Proctor who built [[Fountains Hall]] probably between 1598 and 1604. The hall is a Jacobean mansion, built partly with stone from the Abbey ruins.<ref name=":1" /> Proctor was subsequently imprisoned and sold Fountains Park to pay his legal fees.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|44–45}} In April 1622 the Fountains estate was re-combined by Timothy Whittingham, who re-mortgaged it the same year to Humphrey Wharton. Over the subsequent two years, parts of the estate were ceded to several creditors, but ultimately Wharton regained control. The 1627 estate sale includes details for a lead casting workshop in the Warming House; the estate was bought by Richard Ewens and his son-in-law John Messenger.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=59–60}} During the [[English Civil War]] Messenger reputedly fought at the Battles of [[Battle of Marston Moor|Marston Moor]] and at [[Battle of Naseby|Naseby]]. In 1655 Ewens' grandson, William Messenger, inherited the estate.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=62}}

The Messengers were never wealthy, and in 1676 William Messenger had to arrange mortgages on the estate in order to pay for his daughters' marriages. Other financial troubles led William to leave his family, and he died in Paris in 1680, leaving his three-year-old son, John Messenger, to inherit.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=185}} He married [[Margaret Scrope]] in 1698, a year after he came of age, and around this time he began re-building works on Fountain Hall. He rented out areas of the former abbey, including the mill;{{refn|Fountains Abbey Mill is intact as a building, and is the oldest surviving monastic [[Gristmill|corn mill]] in Europe.<ref name="Coppack">{{cite book |last=Coppack |first=Glyn |title=Fountains Abbey |publisher=Amberley |year=2009 |isbn=978-1-84868-418-8 |page=122}}</ref>|name=first|group=note}} however these leases excluded mineral extraction, which were kept by Messenger. The family were also keenly interested in the ruins of the abbey itself, and allowed people to visit from as early as 1655.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=186–187}}

In 1736, William's son Michael James married Elizabeth Sayer and took responsibility for the estate. He commissioned the first measured survey of the abbey in 1758. The family's financial position was declining and by 1765 Michael was selling oaks from the estate. Michael James died in 1766 and his son John Michael inherited. On 22 December 1767, John Michael sold the Fountains estate to William Aislabie for £18,000.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=195}}

=== Studley estate development ===
[[File:Studley Park, Yorkshire, drawn by J.P. Neale engraved by F. P. Hay.jpg|thumb|Studley Park, Yorkshire, engraved by F. P. Hay after a drawing by [[John Preston Neale]], {{circa|1820}}]]
From 1452 onwards, Studley Royal was inhabited by the Mallory family, most notably by MPs [[John Mallory]] and [[William Mallory]].<ref name="Sir John Mallory of Studley MP2">{{cite web |title=MALLORY, JOHN |url=https://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1604-1629/member/mallory-sir-john-15556-1619 |access-date=5 June 2015 |website=History of Parliament Online}}</ref> A depiction of the enclosed park first appeared on [[Christopher Saxton]]'s 1577 map of Yorkshire.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|44}} In 1607 John Mallory commissioned the first surviving survey of the estate. This listed land-holdings and it demonstrated that the estate formerly extended beyond the park.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|57}} During the English Civil War, William Mallory and his son John, were loyal to the Crown; John commanded a force that defended [[Skipton Castle]]. They only surrendered in December 1645.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=61}} William died in 1646 and John was fined by Parliament for half the value of the estate. Paying off the fine was attempted by selling off his wife's family estate, as well as other property, including a mill at [[Galphay]] and a farm at [[Nunwick]]. However, despite this, when John died in January 1656, and his son William inherited, aged only eight years old, debts had mounted up to £10,000. During this time it was John's widow, Mary, who managed the estate and managed to bring it within its means once more. However, in 1667, William died aged nineteen and the estate passed to his sisters: the eldest Mary, who was married to George Aislabie, as well as Jane and Elizabeth.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=63}}
[[File:East Gate, Studley Royal (geograph 2243765).jpg|thumb|Great Gate, or East Gate, Studley Royal]]
Aislabie was the son of a farmer from Osgodby in North Yorkshire. He worked as a clerk for William Turbutt in the church courts at York. As part of Turbutt's household, Aislabie inherited £200 at his death in November 1648. After Turbutt's death Aislabie remained as part of the household, working for the widowed Elizabeth Turbutt. There is a suggestion made by [[John Richard Walbran]] that the pair may have had a romantic attachment, but this is unproven. Nevertheless he was the primary beneficiary of Turbutt's will when she died in 1662 – a result of which he purchased [[Treasurer's House, York|Treasurer's House]] in York. It was around this time that he married Mary Mallorie. George was killed in a duel in 1676.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=66–68}} It was George who began plans for the re-establishment of an enlarged park with Studley Royal in its centre. [[Studley Great Gate]], now more commonly known as East Gate, the largest probable remnant of his plans.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=75}}

George Aislabie's wife, Mary, preserved the estate, but by the time of her death in 1683 was in debt. Trustees to the estate were appointed until the heir, Mallorie Aislabie, came of age: William Robinson, husband of the eldest daughter Mary and Arthur Ingram.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=69}} Mallorie died in 1685 and was succeeded as heir by his brother George, who inherited but then died in 1693. The third brother, [[John Aislabie]] inherited the Studley estate.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=70}} A survey in 1694 describes both an 'old park' and a 'new park' which invites consideration of expansion under his father.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=79}}

By 1695 John Aislabie was the [[Tories (British political party)|Tory]] [[Ripon (UK Parliament constituency)|Member of Parliament for Ripon]], and in 1718 became [[Chancellor of the Exchequer]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=72}} This enabled some of the family's perhaps long held plans, to landscape the park, to begin. This included the construction of a tower on How Hill, and the canal and cascade that became the foundation of the Water Garden.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=83}} Aislabie was a principal sponsor of the [[South Sea Company]] scheme, the bill for which was promoted by him personally. In 1720 this vast financial operation collapsed, and in 1721 he was expelled from [[Parliament of Great Britain|Parliament]] and disqualified for life from public office. Stocks from the South Sea Company were grafted to the [[East India Company]], of which his brother William was a director.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=87–88}}

=== Development of the gardens ===
[[File:Weir at Studley Royal - geograph.org.uk - 445107.jpg|thumb|Fishing lodges and cascade]]
Aislabie returned to Yorkshire and from 1723 devoted himself to the landscaping of the estate. This included the construction of a boundary wall along the western side of the valley between the Fountains Abbey and Studley Royal estates. This wall had at least one viewing platform and connected to the path known as High Walk. Boundaries including [[ha-ha]]s were also constructed at this time.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=96–97}} It is possible the design was influenced by his kinsman [[William Benson (architect)|William Benson's]] knowledge of [[Palladian architecture|neo-Palladian]] design.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Fry |first=Carole |date=2003 |title=Spanning the Political Divide: Neo-Palladianism and the Early Eighteenth-Century Landscape |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1587294 |journal=Garden History |volume=31 |issue=2 |pages=180–192 |doi=10.2307/1587294 |jstor=1587294 |issn=0307-1243|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Other early features included The Upper Canal and Drum Falls. Flooding subsequently damaged these early developments, and by 1726 approximately 100 men were working to create water features, which included canals and ponds.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=99}} The design of the cascade and the fishing lodges is attributed to [[Roger Morris (1695–1749)|Roger Morris]], who worked with [[Colen Campbell]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=100}} The cascade and the canal was described in 1729 by [[Stephen Switzer]] in his volume of engineering, ''Hydrostatics.'' Aislabie and Morris's works did not just extend to the water gardens, but also to other areas of the estate. By 1728 work was also underway on the High Stables, which can still be seen in the deer park.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=102}}
[[File:Fountains Abbey 2016 056.jpg|thumb|Temple of Piety, 2016]]
During the 1730s and 1740s, there were a number of head gardeners employed by Aislabie. William Fisher worked on the estate from at least 1717 to 1732, when he was paid off. He was followed by John Hossack (left 1738), Mathias Mitchell (dismissed 1742) and then James Lockey (died 1744). Another significant employee was Robert Doe, who was a builder, mason and later head gardener too. Doe later worked on building projects for [[Castle Howard]] and for [[Swinton Estate|Swinton Castle]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=104}} This was also a period of expansion, during which Aislabie changed the lease on Mackershaw – making it permanent, rather than leased for agriculture. During this phase, the Grotto was constructed and changes were made to footpaths around its location; the Temple of Piety was also built.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=114}}

In 1738 the first known plan of the gardens was made by [[Sir John Clerk, 2nd Baronet]] of [[Penicuik]], who was visiting them as part of a northern tour. At the same time the [[Octagon Tower]] was also constructed and it was glazed and decorated during 1735; subsequently stuccowork was added by Francesco Vassalli.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=117}} Other buildings constructed at this time include the Bathing House and the Boathouse. The planting at this time was perhaps sparser than that of the estate in the early twenty-first century: eighteenth-century visitors reported seeing bare rock between the trees.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=124}} The late 1730s also saw a revision of the statuary scheme in the gardens, which included the introduction of a statue of [[Neptune (mythology)|Neptune]], centrally located in the Moon Pond.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=125}}

After Aislabie's death on 18 June 1742, his son [[William Aislabie (1700–1781)|William]] inherited, and whilst little is known about William's life, it is known that he set out on a [[Grand Tour]] in 1720, which was thwarted by political turmoil in France.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=137}} In 1724 he married Lady Elizabeth Cecil, with whom he had six children: two sons and four daughters. Elizabeth and two of their daughters died in a [[smallpox]] outbreak in 1733. William's first scheme for the gardens was the construction of a funerary pyramid, modelled on the one at Stowe Gardens.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=140–143}} He also extended the designed landscape further down the Skell Valley and introduced in 1745 a 'Chinese house', inspired by fashion at the time.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=147}}
[[File:Ice Houses, Studley Park - geograph.org.uk - 1627769.jpg|thumb|Ice houses]]
In 1745 William re-married, to Elizabeth Vernon, who was the niece of his step-mother.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=152}} Perhaps, as a result, Studley Hall was renovated, which included the decoration of two rooms in a [[chinoiserie]] style.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=154}} In the 1750s a gardeners house was built near the Kitchen Garden and two ice houses were constructed. A private garden with aviary was also created next to Studley Hall during this period. William also added new architectural features to the park: an obelisk at the western end of the main avenue and the Belvedere, which was a Gothic garden room.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=160–161}} In addition to new buildings, existing ones had a change of character: for example the Temple of Venus had family portraits installed in it and its name changes to that of 'Banqueting House'.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=163}} In the 1750s the network of footpaths around the gardens were also much greater than the modern-day lay-out.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=166}} Overall, William extended the landscaped area in the picturesque romantic style, contrasting with the formality of his father's work. Between them, the two created what is arguably England's most important 18th-century landscape gardens.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=173}}

=== Combined estates ===
On 22 December 1767, William Aislabie purchased the Fountains estate from John Messenger, combining the Studley and Fountains estates.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=195}}

In February 1768, the gardens were flooded, which resulted in repairs and renovations. Subsequently, the reservoir was expanded from a two-acre, to a three-and-a-half-acre capacity. This period also included the insertion of new garden buildings, such as the Green Arch and the White Seat. In addition, new water features were added to the south-east of the gardens.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=201–203}} 1768 also saw Robert Doe, on the instruction of Aislabie, begin to clear and stabilise the abbey ruins; work which continued until at least 1773.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=207}} Part of this scheme of work included the demolition of the Lay Brothers' Cloister. It also included construction: the Gazebo was built under the east Window, which provided visitors with an elevated view of the nave.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=209}}
[[File:Studley Royal - The Surprise View Through The Door Of Anne Boleyn's Seat - geograph.org.uk - 1005953.jpg|thumb|Studley Royal – The Surprise View Through The Door Of Anne Boleyn's Seat ]]
Anne Boleyn's Hill is first named as part of the gardens in 1771, where an antiquarian headless statue looked out on the valley. The statue moved and was restored to its location in 2004. The last mention of the Rotondo and the Coffin Lawn dates to 1775, and it is likely the former was used to infill the latter. The canalisation of the Skell near the Abbey was undertaken in 1773, which used the river as a framing device for the view of the abbey from [[Anne Boleyn's Seat]] (constructed c. 1789–91).<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=204–206}}

William also added ornamentation to parts of the ruins, adding detailing, but most significantly recreating the location of the high altar through the repositioning of medieval tiles found throughout the site. He also added planting to the cloister, in the form of shrubs and flowers. Greater security was also introduced to the abbey, with lockable doors and gates. However, there was contemporary criticism of these changes, in particular from [[William Gilpin (priest)|William Gilpin]], who visited Studley on a tour of the north of England.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=210–211}}

William died on 17 May 1781 in London. The estate passed to his elder daughter, Elizabeth Allanson, after his two sons predeceased him.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=226}} Whilst she spent the majority of her time as owner at her home in Twickenham, Elizabeth did continue her family's civic work in Ripon – donating to the poor, funding streetlighting and building [[Ripon Town Hall]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=234–235}} However, although she appointed Christopher Hall as agent, during her ownership, and her lack of presence at the estate, many areas of the designed landscape became neglected.<ref>{{cite book |last=McDonagh |first=Briony |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=y3rlvwEACAAJ |title=Elite Women and the Agricultural Landscape, 1700–1830 |date=23 January 2019 |publisher=Taylor & Francis Group |isbn=978-0-367-20821-9 |pages=39 }}</ref><ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=245}}

Elizabeth died on 8 March 1808 and was succeeded by her niece, [[Elizabeth Sophia Lawrence]], who lived at Studley from 1808 until her death in 1845.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=234–235}} An influential woman in the area, she used her position to influence (and punish) voters.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Gleadle |first1=Kathryn |chapter=Introduction: The Petticoat in Politics: Women and Authority |year=2000 |chapter-url=https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-1-349-62989-3_1 |title=Women in British Politics, 1760–1860 |pages=1–18 |editor-last=Gleadle |editor-first=Kathryn |place=London |publisher=Palgrave Macmillan UK |doi=10.1007/978-1-349-62989-3_1 |isbn=978-1-349-62991-6 |access-date=29 December 2022 |last2=Richardson |first2=Sarah |editor2-last=Richardson |editor2-first=Sarah}}</ref> Under her ownership, she made further additions to the grounds, which included [[The Obelisk, Studley Royal|The Obelisk]], as well as Robin Hood's Well, which enclosed a small spring.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=247}} She also made repairs to the abbey, including to Huby's Tower. It was under her ownership that [[John Richard Walbran]] first undertook excavations in 1840.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=254–256}} In her will she left bequests totalling £237,000, including £1000 to fund a dispensary in Ripon.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=238}}

The estate then devolved to [[Thomas de Grey, 2nd Earl de Grey|Thomas Philip, 2nd Earl de Grey]], a distant relative. Under de Grey, Walbran undertook further excavation.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=256}} These excavations in the 1850s also piqued public interest further in the site. This was furthered by the opening of curative springs nearby in [[Harrogate]] bringing a large tourism audience with it.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=273}} Management of the huge increases in the numbers of tourists entailed the creation of new routes, including the De Grey Walk and the Well Walk.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=278}} In 1847 a new one shilling entrance fee was introduced.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=279}} The first record of a school trip to the site was from St Peter's School, [[Dallowgill]], in September 1851.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=280}} In 1858 the first museum for the site was opened in the Muniments Room, above the Warming House.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=267}} The 1850s also saw major events held at the estate for the first time. These raised funds for a variety of causes, such as the Ripon Mechanics Institute, to celebrate [[Treaty of Paris (1856)|peace in Crimea]], and a 'Great Musical Celebration' in 1868. In 1869 the third-earliest bicycle race to be held in England ran through the estate.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=304}}

On de Grey's death in 1859, the estate passed to his nephew, [[George Robinson, 1st Marquess of Ripon]], and later the [[Governor-General of India|Viceroy of India]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=281}} He redeveloped areas of Studley Hall, as well as renovating the gardens near the house. He also constructed the Pheasantry at the edge of the estate, as well as building a golf course, whose first professional was [[Harry Vardon]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=295}} He also built St Mary's church in the park.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=297}} During his ownership of the estate, three structures were added: Studley tea room, an oval island in the lake, and the High Seat in the west of the gardens. During this period, more exotic trees were introduced as part of the planting scheme – for example, a ''[[Sequoiadendron giganteum|Wellingtonia gigantea]]'' was planted by the [[Edward VII|Prince of Wales]] in 1863.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=300}} In 1886 a pageant was held on the estate, celebrating Ripon's millennium; a similar event was repeated in 1896 for the [[Diamond Jubilee of Queen Victoria]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=307}}

The 1st Marquess of Ripon died in 1909 and his son Frederick Oliver inherited the estate. On the death of [[Frederick Robinson, 2nd Marquess of Ripon]] in 1923, the estate was acquired by his cousin Clare George Vyner.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=309–311}} Visitor income became more significant for the Vyner family than it had been for the Robinsons, yet the family had a strong sense of social responsibility and in the 1930s established the [[Fountains Abbey Settlers Society]], which provided work and skills to unemployed families. This also included a work scheme that dredged the river.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=313–314}} In addition to the scheme on the estate, Vyner also funded the construction of a model village in [[Swarland]], Northumberland.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=O’Donnell |first1=Ronan |last2=Petts |first2=David |date=2019 |title='Rural' rhetoric in 1930s unemployment relief schemes |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/rural-history/article/abs/rural-rhetoric-in-1930s-unemployment-relief-schemes/8127B56D6A6C6442BA226D0B2B23B193 |journal=Rural History |volume=30 |issue=1 |pages=53–69 |doi=10.1017/S0956793319000049 |s2cid=166719550 |issn=0956-7933}}</ref> The Settlers Society ended at the outbreak of the Second World War, but its accommodation was repurposed to house German and Polish refugees.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=313-314}} During the war much of the estate's land was cultivated for the first time in 600 years, as part of the '[[Victory garden|Dig For Victory']] campaign.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=324}} Studley Hall after doing war service as the home of [[Queen Ethelburga's Collegiate|Queen Ethelburga's School]], was destroyed by fire in April 1946.<ref>{{cite web |title=Fountains Hall |url=http://www.yorkshireguides.com/fountains_hall.html |access-date=19 January 2023 |website=www.yorkshireguides.com}}</ref> After the war, the upkeep of the estate became too expensive for the Vyner family, who sold it to Broadlands Properties for £1,250,000. They subsequently also sold Fountains Hall to [[West Riding County Council]] in 1969.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=313–314}}

=== Public ownership ===
[[File:Curved roof - geograph.org.uk - 1731436.jpg|thumb|Visitor centre]]
In 1966 much of the estate was purchased by West Riding County Council from Broadlands Properties, and the property was reopened to the public in 1967. However, resource pressures meant that the local authority was keen to find another owner for the estate. There had been previous appeals to the [[National Trust]] to undertake running the site, including in 1923. In 1983 the property was acquired by the National Trust.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=327–329}}

[[English Heritage]] is responsible for the conservation of the abbey under a guardianship agreement, but managed on a day-to-day basis by the National Trust.<ref>{{cite web |title=Studley Royal Park including the Ruins of Fountains Abbey |url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/372/ |access-date=19 January 2023 |website=UNESCO World Heritage Centre }}</ref> St Mary’s Church is owned by the State and managed by the National Trust under a local management agreement.<ref name="SMC"/> In 1986 the entire estate was designated a [[World Heritage Site]] by [[UNESCO]]. It gained recognition as it fulfils the criteria of “being a masterpiece of human creative genius'', and'' an outstanding example of a type of building or architectural or technological ensemble or landscape which illustrates significant stages in human history”.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=328–329}} The initial proposal for World Heritage Status only extended to Fountains Abbey and St Mary's Church; it was on the recommendation of the [[International Council on Monuments and Sites]] (ICOMOS) that the listing extended to include Studley Royal.<ref>France, Sarah, and Natural England Palaeontology. [https://www.academia.edu/download/65416437/Culturally_natural_or_naturally_cultural_Final.pdf#page=26 "“In All, Let Nature Never be Forgot” Integrating the management of natural and cultural values at Studley Royal Park including the ruins of Fountains Abbey World Heritage Site"] ''Culturally Natural or Naturally Cultural'' (2017): 30–34.</ref> In 1992, a new visitor centre and car parks were designed by [[Ted Cullinan]] to accommodate growing visitor numbers. Lying north-west of the Abbey above the valley floor, the new visitor centre incorporated a shop, large restaurant, lecture theatre and exhibition space (currently office space) arranged around an open courtyard.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=|pages=328–329}}

In 2015 stage designer [[Gary McCann]] was commissioned to produce work in response to the buildings on the property; the resulting exhibition, entitled Folly!, installed works in spaces such as the Banqueting House.<ref>{{cite web |title=Folly sculpture is a feather in cap for artist |url=https://www.yorkpress.co.uk/news/ryedale/12913798.folly-sculpture-is-a-feather-in-cap-for-artist-gary-mccann/ |access-date=29 December 2022 |website=York Press |date=25 April 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Brown |first=Irene |date=2015 |title=Folly |url=https://eprints.ncl.ac.uk/214206 |accessdate=10 January 2023}}</ref> In 2016, [[Mat Collishaw]] created ''Seria Ludo'' and ''The Pineal Eye'' in the Temple of Piety.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mat Collishaw at Fountains Abbey & Studley Royal • Arts&Heritage |url=https://www.artsandheritage.org.uk/projects/mat-collishaw-at-fountains-abbey-studley-royal/ |access-date=26 January 2023 |website=Arts&Heritage }}</ref> In 2018, Charles Holland, [[Lucy Orta]] and Flea Folly Architects created artworks to reimagine lost follies in the landscape.<ref>{{cite web |date=11 May 2018 |title=Contemporary Follies Open at Fountains Abbey and Studley Royal Park |url=https://www.archdaily.com/894007/contemporary-follies-open-at-fountains-abbey-and-studley-royal-park |access-date=26 January 2023 |website=ArchDaily }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Lucy + Jorge Orta: Gazing Ball 2018 |url=https://ysp.org.uk/art-outdoors/gazing-ball-2018 |access-date=26 January 2023 |website=Yorkshire Sculpture Park }}</ref> In 2021, Steve Messam created three artworks in an exhibition entitled ''These Passing Things'' and in 2022 [[Joe Cornish (photographer)|Joe Cornish]] created a photographic exhibition ''Still Time to Wonder'' in various buildings on the property.<ref>{{cite news |date=10 July 2021 |title=Fountains Abbey: Artwork based on mystery pyramid on display |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-york-north-yorkshire-57789490 |access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Joe Cornish {{!}}Still Time to Wonder {{!}} On Landscape |url=https://www.onlandscape.co.uk/2022/05/still-time-to-wonder/ |access-date=26 January 2023 |website=www.onlandscape.co.uk}}</ref>

=== Significance ===
Studley Royal, under National Trust ownership, is the preserved core of a once much more substantial Aislabie project, which incorporated the surrounding agrarian landscape that they owned, long-distance views to Ripon and beyond, and rides extending to other designed landscapes including [[Laver Banks]] and [[Hack Fall Wood|Hackfall]] (seven miles from Studley).<ref name=":0" /><sup>:171, 177–184</sup> The gardens and park reflect every stage in the evolution of English garden fashion, from the late seventeenth-century to the 1780s and beyond. Most unusually both John and William embraced new garden fashions by extending their [[designed landscape]] rather than replacing and remaking outmoded parts.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:333</sup> As a result, the cumulative whole is a catalogue of significant landscaping styles. This includes John Aislabie's ground-breaking appreciation of natural topographical landforms, for him it was not necessary to level ground and create a garden, the garden could be made to accommodate and display the underlying landscape.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:231</sup>  

==Major features==
{{main|Fountains Abbey}}
{{main|Fountains Hall}}
The park incorporates Fountains Abbey, Fountains Hall, and several other notable historic features.

===Studley Royal Water Garden===
[[File:The Lake, from Temple of Piety, Studley Park, Yorkshire. Published 21 June 1866.jpg|thumb|The Lake, from Temple of Piety, Studley Park, Yorkshire. Published 21 June 1866.]]
{{Main|Fountains Abbey and Studley Royal Water Gardens}}
The water garden at Studley Royal created by [[John Aislabie]] in 1718 is one of the best surviving examples of a [[Georgian era|Georgian]] [[water garden]] in England. It was expanded by his son, William who purchased the adjacent Fountains Estate. The garden's elegant ornamental lakes, [[Canal (garden history)|canals]], temples and cascades provide a succession of dramatic eye-catching vistas. It is also studded with a number of [[Folly|follies]] including a [[Neo Gothic|neo-Gothic]] tower and a [[palladian]]-style [[banqueting house]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Barker |first=Malcolm |date=1968 |title=The Cipher of Studley Royal |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1586200 |journal=Quarterly Newsletter (Garden History Society) |issue=8 |pages=15–18 |doi=10.2307/1586200 |jstor=1586200 |issn=0435-1185|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

The grade I-listed canal is about {{convert|500|m}} long and {{convert|10|m}} wide, and is angled near the [[weir]] called Drum Falls. It has [[gritstone]] walls and a puddled clay base. On the east side is a segmental arch covering a [[sluice]] outlet.<ref>{{NHLE |num= 1173904|desc= Canal approximately 500 metres long, with the Drum Falls and weir inlet, Lindrick with Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 13 April 2025}}</ref><ref name="pevsner">{{cite book| last =Leach| first =Peter| last2 = Pevsner | first2 = Nikolaus | author2-link = Nikolaus Pevsner | series= The Buildings of England| title =Yorkshire West Riding: Leeds, Bradford and the North| publisher =[[Yale University Press]] | year =2009 | location =New Haven and London |isbn =978-0-300-12665-5}}</ref>  The [[weir]] at its north end is grade II* listed along with its piers, pavilions and balustrades.  The weir is built of [[gritstone]] and has a cascade of four steps. It is flanked by the [[pier (architecture)|piers]] with bands of frosted [[rustication (architecture)|rustication]] and ball [[finial]]s. Outside these are the [[balustrade]]d walls leading to fishing [[pavilion]]s over double-arched [[sluice]]s. Each pavilion has a [[Venetian window]], a [[molding (architecture)|moulded]] [[eaves]] [[cornice]], and a pyramidal roof with a ball finial and a [[weathervane]]. Each [[revetment]] wall has a stone mask and a water spout with a stone basin.<ref name="pevsner" /><ref>{{NHLE |num= 1173821|desc= Weir at north end of the Canal, with piers, fishing pavilions and balustrade, Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 17 April 2025}}</ref>

The Half-Moon Pond is grade II* listed, as are the Moon and Crescent Ponds.  They have stone walls and are lined in clay. The Moon Pond is a circular with a submerged [[causeway]], and it is flanked by the crescent-shaped ponds.  The Half-Moon Pond has parallel semicircular sides joined by straight sides.<ref name="pevsner" /><ref>{{NHLE |num= 1173896|desc= Half Moon Pond, Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 14 April 2025}}</ref><ref>{{NHLE |num= 1150612|desc= Moon and Crescent Ponds, Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 14 April 2025}}</ref>

===St Mary's Church===
{{main|St Mary's, Studley Royal}}
[[File:St Marys Church, Studley Royal - geograph.org.uk - 7683.jpg|right|thumb|View of the West End of the church]]
St Mary's Church was built by the architect [[William Burges]] and commissioned by the family of the [[George Robinson, 1st Marquess of Ripon|1st Marquess of Ripon]]. It has been suggested that the construction of this place of worship was prompted by the death of Frederick Grantham Vyner, who was kidnapped and killed in Greece in 1870.<ref name="SMC">{{cite web |title=St Mary's Church, Studley Royal |url=https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/st-marys-church-studley-royal/ |access-date=26 January 2023 |publisher=English Heritage}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=The Dilessi Massacre and a Gothic Revival Masterpiece |url=https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/st-marys-church-studley-royal/history/dilessi-massacre/ |access-date=26 January 2023 |publisher=English Heritage}}</ref>

Burges' appointment as architect was most likely due to the connection between his greatest patron, [[John Crichton-Stuart, 3rd Marquess of Bute]] and Vyner, who had been friends at Oxford. St Mary's, on Lady Ripon's estate at Studley Royal, was commissioned in 1870 and work began in 1871. The church was consecrated in 1878. As at Skelton, Burges' design demonstrates a move from his favoured Early-French, to an English style. [[Nikolaus Pevsner]] writes of "a Victorian shrine, a dream of [[Early English period|Early English]] glory." The interior is spectacular, exceeding Skelton in richness and majesty. The stained glass is of particularly high quality. St Mary's is Burges' "ecclesiastical masterpiece."<ref name=":3">{{cite web |title=History and Research: St Mary's Church, Studley Royal |url=https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/daysout/properties/st-marys-church-studley-royal/history-and-research/ |access-date=7 February 2012 |publisher=English Heritage}}</ref>

Both marquesses and their wives are buried there.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=286}}

=== How Hill Tower ===
{{main|How Hill Tower}}
[[File:Chapel on How Hill - geograph.org.uk - 118576.jpg|thumb|How Hill Tower]]
Prior to 1346, a chapel dedicated to [[Michael (archangel)|St Michael the Archangel]] was built on How Hill. This became a minor medieval pilgrimage site.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:28</sup> Post-reformation the chapel continued to be used between 1551–54 for the churching of women, until falling into ruin.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:42</sup>  A tower was constructed next to the ruins, and reusing some of their masonry.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:83–85</sup>

===Deer park===
The deer park, where the church stands, is home to deer, and a wealth of other [[Flora (plants)|flora]] and [[Fauna (animals)|fauna]]. At Studley there are three types of deer: [[Red Deer]], [[Fallow Deer]] and [[Sika Deer]].<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ylAmEAAAQBAJ&dq=studley+royal+deer+park&pg=PT81 |title=Great British Seasonal Walks |date=26 October 2018 |publisher=National Trust |isbn=978-1-911358-57-2 }}</ref> John Clerk, visiting in 1738, described how the buck deer moved in a group, so that they "resemble a moving forrest [sic]".<ref>{{cite journal |last=Brown |first=Ian Gordon |title='A Fitter Place for Campania Than Yorkshire': Sir John Clerk and Thomas Blackwell at Studley Royal |date=2017 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/44987944 |journal=Garden History |volume=45 |issue=1 |pages=66–81 |jstor=44987944 |issn=0307-1243}}</ref>

== Buildings and structures ==

===Studley Royal House===
[[File:Studley Royal from Morriss County Seats (1880).jpg|right|thumb|Studley Royal House in 1880]]
Studley Royal House (or Hall) stood in the north-west corner of the park. Originally a medieval [[manor house]], there is a record from the 1220s of an extensive garden created by Cassandra de Aleman.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:33</sup> It consisted of a main block with forward projecting wings, it burned down in 1716 and was rebuilt by John Aislabie. He filled in the centre, to which his son William added a portico in 1762 to complete its Palladian appearance.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=286–289}}

It was altered and developed by the [[George Robinson, 1st Marquess of Ripon|1st Marquess of Ripon]], who created a new entrance hall, a royal suite, and the reorganisation of the domestic service areas. He also added a Catholic chapel at the western side of the house in 1878.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=292–294}}

The house burnt down in 1946, and its remains were demolished by the Vyner family, who could not afford repairs to it. Instead, the splendid Georgian stable block, built for John Aislabie’s racehorses between 1728 and 1732, was converted into an elegant Palladian country house.  It has adoped the name [[Studley Royal House]] and is set in 2½ acres of private formal gardens on high ground overlooking the deer park towards Ripon Cathedral in the distance.<ref name="Churchill">{{cite magazine|last=Churchill|first=Penny|date=7 December 2023|title=A spectacular mansion for sale in the heart of a World Heritage Site — and it's something of a bargain|magazine=Country Life|url=https://www.countrylife.co.uk/property/the-spectacular-8-million-mansion-for-sale-thats-in-the-heart-of-a-world-heritage-site-256248|accessdate=11 January 2024}}</ref>

Built of stone under a slate roof with distinctive pavilion towers in each of the four corners, the pristine, 11,708sq ft house surrounds a central square courtyard overlooked by all the main rooms and dominated by the working clock tower.<ref name="Churchill" />

=== Studley Magna ===
[[File:Ridge and furrow in Studley Park - geograph.org.uk - 1626460.jpg|thumb|Ridge and furrow in Studley Park – likely associated with Studley Magna]]
The [[deserted medieval village]] of [[Studley Magna]] mainly lies within the boundaries of the park.<ref>{{cite web |title=Heritage Gateway – Results |url=https://www.heritagegateway.org.uk/Gateway/Results_Single.aspx?uid=1093430&resourceID=19191 |access-date=28 November 2022 |website=www.heritagegateway.org.uk}}</ref> Excavation demonstrated that the village was aligned with the important road to [[Aldfield]]. The earliest ceramics from the site date to c.1180–1220, whilst the latest finds date from c.1300. The site included a large two-storey miller's house with a stone fireplace that was rare for the period. The house was sold in 1362 by Widow Horner to Richard Tempest.<ref name=":0" /><sup>:30–32</sup>

=== The Banqueting House ===
[[File:The Banqueting House - geograph.org.uk - 1626503.jpg|thumb|Banqueting House]]
Documentary evidence suggests that the Banqueting House was being completed in 1731, and is described in the estate accounts as 'the new building'. The structure had several phases of alteration after construction, and other documents refer to it as 'the Greenhouse'. In front of the façade a deep coffin-shaped lawn was introduced, who sightlines connected the building and the Rotondo. Above the Banqueting House, amongst the trees, is an oval platform, which is likely to be the Dial Lawn, which is described in accounts in 1730s.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=109–110}}  It is a [[grade I listed building]].<ref name="nhlebanqueting">{{NHLE |num= 1150608|desc= Banqueting House Approximately 100 Metres to west of the Canal, Lindrick with Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 13 April 2025}}</ref><ref name="pevsner" />

The building is constructed of stone with a [[hip roof|hipped]] [[Westmorland]] [[slate]] roof, one storey and three [[bay (architecture)|bays]]. In the centre is a doorway with a round-arched [[fanlight]], flanked by round-arched [[sash window]]s. Each opening has [[rustication (architecture)|rusticated]] [[voussoir]]s, and a [[keystone (architecture)|keystone]] with a mask, and under each window is a [[balustrade]]. Six [[pilaster]]s with banded rustication carry a [[cornice]] and a balustraded [[parapet]] with ball [[finial]]s. On the sides are rusticated [[apse|apsidal]] projections with domed roofs.<ref name="nhlebanqueting" />

=== The Temple of Piety ===
The building was constructed based on a sketch by [[Andrea Palladio]] owned by [[Richard Boyle, 3rd Earl of Burlington]], and was initially known as the Temple of Hercules. Documentary evidence shows that it was constructed and named by April 1736.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=116}}  It is also grade I listed.<ref name="nhlepiety">{{NHLE |num= 1150613|desc= Temple of Piety on east side of Moon Pond, Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 16 April 2025}}</ref>

The temple is in [[stucco|rendered]] brick and stone, and has a [[Westmorland]] [[slate]] roof with lead verges. On the front is a [[portico]] of six [[Doric order|Roman Doric]] columns on a [[gritstone]] pavement, with an [[entablature]], a [[pediment]] and a [[pedestal]]. The central doorway has an [[architrave]] with a mask [[keystone (architecture)|keystone]], and is flanked by pairs of windows in architraves. In each of the returns is a doorway in an eared surround with a triple keystone.<ref name="nhlepiety" /><ref name="pevsner" />

=== Mackershaw Lodges ===
These buildings were constructed after 1731, with the change in terms of the loan (to acquisition) of the Mackershaw area. They compromise two small lodges with classical pediments either side of a central arch, constructed from rough, undressed stone, with Venetian windows.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=121}}  The gateway and flanking lodges are in [[limestone]], and are in ruins. The central round-arched gateway has massive [[voussoir]]s and a [[keystone (architecture)|keystone]], and is flanked by flat-headed pedestrian gateways, over which are round-arched recesses. Above these is a [[modillion]] [[eaves]] [[cornice]], and three bases for statues. The flanking lodges each has a [[Venetian window]] with an [[impost (architecture)|impost]] band, a modillion eaves cornice and a [[pediment]].  The structure is listed at grade II.<ref>{{NHLE |num= 1150617|desc= Mackershaw Lodge on south side of Studley Park, Studley Royal and Fountains|access-date= 14 April 2025}}</ref>

=== Lost buildings ===

==== Wattle Hall ====
One of the buildings most frequently attested in the early eighteenth-century is the Wattle Hall. Surviving records suggest that it was made of bent branches rather than brick or stone, and it was repaired in 1732.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=98}}

==== Rotondo ====
Close to Kendall's Walk and in the north-east corner of Coffin Lawn, evidence for the Rotondo first appears in a painting dating to 1734–41. It was demolished in the 1770s. A close comparison to this would have been the Temple of Venus in [[Stowe Gardens]].<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=108}}

==== Pyramid ====
William's first building work for the gardens was ordering the construction of a funerary pyramid, modelled on the one at Stowe Gardens. Whilst designs for this building exist, its location is unknown. It is possible that the stone was cut, but it was never constructed.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|pages=141–143}}

==== Chinese house ====
[[File:Bridleway into Chinese Wood - geograph.org.uk - 3240990.jpg|thumb|Bridleway into Chinese Wood]]
The Chinese house was constructed in 1745 and a 1751 visitor described it as having blue columns, gilded decoration, a white ceiling, a variety of Chinese ornaments and stuccowork by [[Giuseppe Cortese]]. It also had a balustrade seat running inside the columns.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=147}} There is a surviving sketch of the building, but only the plinth for its survives. It was located beyond the southern limit of National Trust estate.<ref name=":0" />{{Rp|page=151}} The area around the house was known as the Chinese Wood, to which two chinoiserie-style bridges provided access.<ref>Fekete, Albert, and Peter Gyori. [https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Peter-Gyori/publication/355187688_Chinese_pavilions_in_the_early_landscape_gardens_of_Europe/links/6165adf41eb5da761e878791/Chinese-pavilions-in-the-early-landscape-gardens-of-Europe.pdf "Chinese pavilions in the early landscape gardens of Europe."] ''Landscape architecture and art: scientific journal of Latvia University of Life Sciences and Technologies (Latvia)'' (2021).</ref>

